WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.412
This is the first real retrieval step.

00:00:02.412 --> 00:00:06.847
We embed the user's question with the same model used for the chunks.

00:00:06.847 --> 00:00:13.362
Because the vectors are normalized, the dot product gives us a cosine-style similarity score.

00:00:13.362 --> 00:00:19.312
Then we sort the scores from highest to lowest and return the top matching chunk rows.

00:00:19.312 --> 00:00:22.927
I want you to notice that there is no vector database yet.

00:00:22.927 --> 00:00:26.739
We can understand the full retrieval logic with a NumPy matrix.

00:00:27.189 --> 00:00:33.596
That is useful because later, when Chroma, Qdrant, or another database performs the search,

00:00:33.596 --> 00:00:37.141
you will know what job the database is replacing.

00:00:37.141 --> 00:00:42.651
Also notice that the function returns metadata along with text and score.

00:00:42.651 --> 00:00:49.841
The retrieval result should be useful to the rest of the application, not just a list of vector positions.

00:00:49.841 --> 00:00:52.993
This simple function is our baseline retriever.
